Three author rulings of 2026-08-30, and the try-with-resources hole they sit next to:

- **Q11/I20.** A call that leaves a clause-only type parameter to inference is rejected. Reading
  the solver first settled what "the language does in general": 'unable to infer' fires only for a
  variable with NO constraints, while a 'where T <: Exception' bound seeds the constraint set and
  the variable is solved AT ITS BOUND -- so 'g(0, f)' compiling was ordinary language behaviour,
  and the fix has to be a dedicated check, never a solver change. 'CollectClauseOnlyTyVars' is the
  difference of two walks (capability-aware minus erased); the check runs in 'PostCheckCallExpr',
  after the candidate is fixed, so overload resolution is untouched. Only type arguments the
  AUTHOR wrote discharge it -- consulting the solved 'instTys' would consult the very choice the
  rule rejects. One diagnostic per call, since the diagnostic set deduplicates by position.

- **Q10/I18.** With BOTH capability features off, clauses shape no type: 'capTys' are dropped at
  the single 'GetFunctionTy' funnel, so '() throws E -> R' IS '() -> R' -- one identity, assignable
  in every position, absent from the '.cjo'. 'throws' never affected overloading; now it does not
  affect subtyping either, which is the ruling.

- **Q8 residue.** The implicit 'close()' of try-with-resources demanded nothing at all: the
  capability pass runs before desugar, where those calls do not exist yet. Each resource's
  effective close list (declared, instantiated through the resource type, plus what inference gave
  it) is now demanded inside the try's own scope -- which a probe confirmed is where the
  desugaring puts the closing. Inert while 'Resource.close()' is clause-free, and that is exactly
  why it lands before the stdlib gives it one.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
